--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Some components carry obligations that are triggered by making the software
available to other people over a network, not by distributing it:

MinIO and MinIO Client (AGPL-3.0), section 13
  If you modify MinIO and make the modified version available to users over a
  network, you must offer those users the corresponding source code of your
  modified version. Running the unmodified image as shipped does not create
  that obligation.

MongoDB (SSPL-1.0), section 13
  If you offer the functionality of MongoDB to third parties as a service, the
  SSPL requires you to release the source code of the entire service
  management stack you use to do so. Using MongoDB as the database behind your
  own application does not trigger this. Offering a database service built on
  it does.

Redis
  If the version listed in section 1 is under RSALv2, that license prohibits
  offering the software to third parties as a managed service and prohibits
  circumventing license functionality. If it is under SSPLv1, the section 13
  obligation described above applies.

These obligations fall on whoever operates the deployment. If you hold a
PrivOS Partner License and offer PrivOS as a hosted or managed service, the
Partner License covers the PrivOS software only. It does not and cannot
relieve you of the obligations of these third-party licenses; review them for
